@Parameters in Java, particularly in the context of TestNG
Basic Questions
	1. What is the purpose of @Parameters in TestNG?
		○ @Parameters is used to pass values from the testng.xml file to your test methods, constructors, or configuration methods (like @BeforeTest, @BeforeClass, etc.).
	2. How do you use @Parameters in a TestNG test method?
@Test
@Parameters("browser")
public void testSetup(String browser) {
    System.out.println("Browser passed: " + browser);
}
	3. How do you define parameters in the testng.xml file?
<suite name="Suite1">
    <test name="Test1">
        <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>
        <classes>
            <class name="tests.MyTestClass"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>

Intermediate Questions
	1. What is the difference between @Parameters and @DataProvider in TestNG?
		○ @Parameters: Passes single or static values from XML.
		○ @DataProvider: Supplies multiple sets of data programmatically, often for data-driven testing.
	2. Can @Parameters be used with @BeforeClass or @BeforeTest?
		○ Yes, you can use it with any method that accepts parameters, including @BeforeSuite, @BeforeTest, @BeforeClass, etc.
@BeforeTest
@Parameters("url")
public void setup(String url) {
    driver.get(url);
}
	3. What happens if the parameter is not defined in testng.xml?
		○ TestNG will throw an exception like:
org.testng.TestNGException: Parameter 'browser' is required but was not found in the suite.
 Advanced or Practical Questions
	1. How can you pass multiple parameters using @Parameters?
@Test
@Parameters({"username", "password"})
public void loginTest(String user, String pass) {
    System.out.println(user + " | " + pass);
}
	2. Can @Parameters be used together with @DataProvider?
		○ Not directly. @Parameters is static and comes from XML; @DataProvider is dynamic. You typically choose one based on use case.
	3. How do you pass parameters to constructors in TestNG using @Parameters?
public class MyTest {
    private String browser;

@Parameters("browser")
    public MyTest(String browser) {
        this.browser = browser;
    }

@Test
    public void testMethod() {
        System.out.println("Running test on: " + browser);
    }
}

Note: This works only if you're using a factory or configuring the constructor use properly.

Real-World Usage Scenario
	1. How can @Parameters help manage different environments (dev, qa, prod)?
	• You can define an env parameter in testng.xml, and use it in your setup methods to load different config files or URLs.
<parameter name="env" value="qa"/>
@BeforeTest
@Parameters("env")
public void setupEnv(String env) {
    if (env.equals("qa")) {
        baseUrl = "https://qa.example.com";
    } else if (env.equals("prod")) {
        baseUrl = "https://prod.example.com";
    }
}

